  "name": null,
  "problem": "It can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${x}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 6-12{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6 & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${x}^{\u2032}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$",
